{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-5985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{1+25{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=25{x}^{4}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 5\\int {x}^{2}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7cb11605217c1c6bb1b419f93461fc14a60161bb4fe0cc680ff39d9347b1e3"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}"] ["math" "y=10x-8"] ["math" "y=5{x}^{2}"] ["math" "y=5{x}^{5}+\\frac{9}{5}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+x+\\frac{12}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-5985" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{11}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-120752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}$ for $x\\in [\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},1].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-120752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}},$ $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x){|}_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-120752" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7cb11605217c1c6bb1b419f93461fc14a60161bb4fe0cc680ff39d9347b1e3"} {"question" "q-120752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-120752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-120752" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [0,3].$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{(1+{x}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x=2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[2x\\sqrt{(1+{x}^{2})}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=2{x}^{2}+1$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+x)|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3}+(3)]-[\\frac{2}{3}(0)^{3}+(0)] \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7cb11605217c1c6bb1b419f93461fc14a60161bb4fe0cc680ff39d9347b1e3"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "15"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "25"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122287" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{(2-x)^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{2-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{2-x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm (-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x))+C \\\\  & =∓\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7cb11605217c1c6bb1b419f93461fc14a60161bb4fe0cc680ff39d9347b1e3"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)+1"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+1"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+x)+1"] ["math" "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-122390" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-362590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Compute the arc length of $y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,1].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-362590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}=-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1+x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{(1+x)^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1+2x+{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2+2x}{1-{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2(1+x)}{(1-x)(1+x)}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{1-x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-x}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-1}-(-2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1-0}) \\\\  & =0-(-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-362590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9a7cb11605217c1c6bb1b419f93461fc14a60161bb4fe0cc680ff39d9347b1e3"} {"question" "q-362590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"} {"question" "q-362590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029846/state.json" "file_sha256" "e36ee18533da267c865506af676af0aae051666d69933394f81a66f72fdb2f78"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1201 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
